1
00:00:00,300 --> 00:00:01,480
Listen closely.

2
00:00:01,230 --> 00:00:03,473
That’s the sound of a heart that has never

3
00:00:03,223 --> 00:00:04,280
needed a battery.

4
00:00:04,030 --> 00:00:05,777
Four beats every second,

5
00:00:05,527 --> 00:00:07,462
for as long as you wear it.

6
00:00:08,162 --> 00:00:10,562
This is calibre 3235.

7
00:00:10,312 --> 00:00:12,281
It beats inside the Datejust,

8
00:00:12,031 --> 00:00:14,652
the Submariner Date and the Sea-Dweller.

9
00:00:14,402 --> 00:00:16,212
From the outside, it’s calm.

10
00:00:15,962 --> 00:00:17,518
But turn it over…

11
00:00:18,919 --> 00:00:19,861
Open it up,

12
00:00:19,611 --> 00:00:22,567
and you’ll find something rather wonderful.

13
00:00:24,867 --> 00:00:25,857
No battery.

14
00:00:25,607 --> 00:00:26,707
No circuitry.

15
00:00:26,457 --> 00:00:28,339
Just springs, wheels and jewels,

16
00:00:28,089 --> 00:00:29,717
every one with a job to do.

17
00:00:29,467 --> 00:00:31,010
So let’s take it apart,

18
00:00:30,760 --> 00:00:31,853
layer by layer.

19
00:00:33,203 --> 00:00:34,585
Lift the layers apart,

20
00:00:34,335 --> 00:00:36,283
and the movement becomes a stack.

21
00:00:36,033 --> 00:00:37,139
The rotor on top,

22
00:00:36,889 --> 00:00:39,657
then bridges set with thirty-one synthetic rubies,

23
00:00:39,407 --> 00:00:41,973
tiny bearings that keep friction to a whisper.

24
00:00:41,723 --> 00:00:42,862
Then the balance,

25
00:00:42,612 --> 00:00:44,484
the escapement, the gear train,

26
00:00:44,234 --> 00:00:46,105
the barrel, and the main plate.

27
00:00:47,805 --> 00:00:50,097
It starts with the Perpetual rotor,

28
00:00:49,847 --> 00:00:51,614
patented by Rolex in 1931,

29
00:00:51,364 --> 00:00:53,540
and still one of the most elegant

30
00:00:53,290 --> 00:00:54,765
ideas in watchmaking.

31
00:00:54,515 --> 00:00:56,271
A half-moon of dense metal,

32
00:00:56,021 --> 00:00:58,335
it spins on a ball bearing every time

33
00:00:58,085 --> 00:00:59,283
your wrist moves,

34
00:00:59,033 --> 00:01:02,462
and reversing wheels turn every swing into winding force.

35
00:01:03,162 --> 00:01:05,471
That winding flows into the mainspring,

36
00:01:05,221 --> 00:01:06,792
coiled inside the barrel.

37
00:01:06,542 --> 00:01:08,560
Rolex gave the barrel thinner walls,

38
00:01:08,310 --> 00:01:10,182
leaving room for a longer spring.

39
00:01:09,932 --> 00:01:12,982
Fully wound, it holds around 70 hours of power.

40
00:01:12,732 --> 00:01:14,476
Take it off on Friday evening,

41
00:01:14,226 --> 00:01:15,671
and come Monday morning,

42
00:01:15,421 --> 00:01:16,618
it’s still running.

43
00:01:17,317 --> 00:01:19,329
The spring lets that energy out slowly,

44
00:01:19,079 --> 00:01:20,367
through the gear train.

45
00:01:20,117 --> 00:01:22,073
The centre wheel turns once an hour

46
00:01:21,823 --> 00:01:23,047
for the minute hand.

47
00:01:22,797 --> 00:01:24,827
The fourth wheel turns once a minute

48
00:01:24,577 --> 00:01:25,617
for the seconds.

49
00:01:25,367 --> 00:01:27,910
Left alone, this train would simply race.

50
00:01:28,610 --> 00:01:30,380
So the escapement holds it back.

51
00:01:30,130 --> 00:01:32,633
The pallet fork catches the escape wheel,

52
00:01:32,383 --> 00:01:34,062
lets it slip half a tooth,

53
00:01:33,812 --> 00:01:36,040
and gives the balance a gentle push.

54
00:01:35,790 --> 00:01:37,540
Lock, release, impulse.

55
00:01:37,290 --> 00:01:38,565
That’s the tick you hear.

56
00:01:39,166 --> 00:01:42,181
Rolex's Chronergy escapement is around fifteen per

57
00:01:41,931 --> 00:01:44,726
cent more efficient than a conventional lever.

58
00:01:44,476 --> 00:01:47,242
And because it's made of nickel-phosphorus,

59
00:01:46,992 --> 00:01:48,939
magnetism can't throw it off.

60
00:01:49,639 --> 00:01:51,689
And here is the heart of the watch.

61
00:01:51,439 --> 00:01:52,607
The balance wheel,

62
00:01:52,357 --> 00:01:54,289
swinging four times every second.

63
00:01:54,039 --> 00:01:55,875
That's twenty-eight thousand,

64
00:01:55,625 --> 00:01:57,407
eight hundred beats an hour,

65
00:01:57,157 --> 00:01:59,650
on a blue Parachrom hairspring of niobium

66
00:01:59,400 --> 00:02:01,893
and zirconium, that shrugs off magnetism,

67
00:02:01,643 --> 00:02:02,659
heat and cold.

68
00:02:02,409 --> 00:02:05,786
And Paraflex shock absorbers guard its delicate pivots.

69
00:02:07,236 --> 00:02:08,437
On the dial side,

70
00:02:08,187 --> 00:02:10,394
the motion works turn the hour hand

71
00:02:10,144 --> 00:02:12,856
at one-twelfth the speed of the minute hand.

72
00:02:12,606 --> 00:02:14,586
And at midnight, the date doesn't creep.

73
00:02:14,336 --> 00:02:14,906
It jumps!

74
00:02:14,656 --> 00:02:15,594
Instantly.

75
00:02:17,494 --> 00:02:18,974
Put it all back together.

76
00:02:18,724 --> 00:02:19,821
The rotor winds,

77
00:02:19,571 --> 00:02:20,775
the spring stores,

78
00:02:20,525 --> 00:02:21,675
the wheels count,

79
00:02:21,425 --> 00:02:22,945
the escapement releases,

80
00:02:22,695 --> 00:02:24,375
and the balance keeps time.

81
00:02:24,775 --> 00:02:27,146
Rolex tests every cased watch to within

82
00:02:26,896 --> 00:02:28,125
two seconds a day.

83
00:02:27,875 --> 00:02:30,705
They call it the Superlative Chronometer standard.

84
00:02:30,455 --> 00:02:32,247
And it does all of that without

85
00:02:31,997 --> 00:02:33,390
ever needing a battery.

86
00:02:35,840 --> 00:02:38,020
If you enjoyed this, subscribe.

87
00:02:37,770 --> 00:02:40,881
Next time, we’ll open up another legendary movement.

